### Summary

`surrealdb-core` pins `reblessive 0.4.3` (crates.io, checksum `bbc4a4ea…e73c8c`) and uses its recursive-async runtime for every SurrealQL statement: `dbs::Executor` holds a `TreeStack` field and drives each statement through `self.stack.enter(|stk| s.compute(stk, &self.ctx, &self.opt, None)).finish().await`. `reblessive 0.4.3` unconditionally asserts `Send`/`Sync` for its runtime types (`unsafe impl Send/Sync for Stack`, `unsafe impl Send/Sync for TreeStack`, `unsafe impl<R> Send/Sync for Runner`), while its public entry points accept **any** future with no `Send` bound (`pub fn enter<'a, F, Fut, R>(&'a mut self, f: F) -> Runner<'a, R> where F: FnOnce(&'a mut Stk) -> Fut, Fut: Future<Output = R> + 'a`) and store it type-erased (function-pointer vtable) in a bump allocator. Two safe-code consequences follow: (1) `Runner::step_async`/`finish_async` return `StepFuture<'a, R>`/`FinishFuture<'a, R>` whose lifetime is the stack borrow rather than `&mut self`, so driver futures can be duplicated; because `Stack: Sync`, those futures are `Send`, and two threads polling them run `drive_top_task(&self)` concurrently - racy `Cell`/`UnsafeCell` reads and writes, double `pop_task()`/`pop_dealloc()` (double drop / double free) and a non-atomic `Option<R>::take`; (2) `TreeStack: Send` lets safe code move a driver whose type-erased future captures `!Send` state (`Rc`, `RefCell`) to another thread while the creating thread keeps an alias of that state. ThreadSanitizer confirms 11 data races on the attached PoC (including on `reblessive`'s own `Cell<usize>`/`StackAllocator` state) and the corrupted allocator makes `reblessive` itself panic (`stack/mod.rs:177:54`, `Option::unwrap()` on `None`) in one case. No `unsafe` is needed at the call site. SurrealDB's own query futures stay `Send` only because of the unsound `unsafe impl Send for TreeStack`: the futures stored in `Executor`'s `TreeStack` capture `&mut tree::Stk`, and `tree::Stk` is deliberately `!Send` (`PhantomData<*mut TreeStack>`).

### Details

**Verified snapshot.** `surrealdb 3.3.0-nightly`, git commit `18971ffba781a796c0ad3897bb4b82432c241e4c` (2026-09-04), crate `surrealdb-core 3.3.0-nightly`. Dependency `reblessive 0.4.3` (crates.io, checksum `bbc4a4ea2a66a41a1152c4b3d86e8954dc087bdf33af35446e6e176db4e73c8c`), declared at workspace `Cargo.toml:94` and `surrealdb/core/Cargo.toml:95` (`reblessive = { workspace = true, features = ["tree"] }`). We verified only this snapshot; any release that resolves `reblessive 0.4.3` with these sources is affected - please set the final affected/patched ranges when publishing. As of 2026-09-21, crates.io's newest `reblessive` is still 0.4.3 and the upstream repository (`github.com/DelSkayn/reblessive`, maintained by a SurrealDB core developer) still carries the same `unsafe impl`s on its `main` branch; no fix is staged.

**Root cause.** The runtime asserts `Send`/`Sync` unconditionally while storing type-erased futures that have no `Send` bound:

- `unsafe impl Send for Stack {}` / `unsafe impl Sync for Stack {}` - `reblessive-0.4.3/src/stack/mod.rs:65-66`; the struct (`:58-63`) holds `UnsafeCell<StackAllocator>` plus `Cell<usize> len`, `Cell<StackState> state`, `Cell<usize> async_context` (all non-synchronised).
- `unsafe impl Send for TreeStack {}` / `unsafe impl Sync for TreeStack {}` - `reblessive-0.4.3/src/tree/mod.rs:34-35`.
- `unsafe impl<R> Send for Runner<'_, R> {}` / `unsafe impl<R> Sync for Runner<'_, R> {}` - `reblessive-0.4.3/src/tree/runner.rs:64-65`.
- The public entry points take any future, with **no `Send` bound**: `src/stack/mod.rs:82-90` and `src/tree/mod.rs:45-53`. The future is moved into `TaskBox { v_table: VTable::get::<F>(), future: f }` (`src/stack/mod.rs:139-151`) and driven through monomorphised function pointers (`src/vtable.rs:25-60`, `Pin::new_unchecked(...).poll(ctx)`).
- Driver futures outlive the `&mut Runner` borrow: `pub fn step_async(&mut self) -> StepFuture<'a, R>` and `pub fn finish_async(&mut self) -> FinishFuture<'a, R>` (`src/stack/runner.rs:24,35`) return the *stack* lifetime `'a`, so several can coexist; `StepFuture`/`FinishFuture` only hold `&'a Stack` (`src/stack/runner.rs:104-107,129-132`), and `Stack: Sync` makes them `Send`.
- `Stack::drive_top_task(&self)` (`src/stack/mod.rs:175-199`) mutates the stack through a shared reference: on `Ready` it runs `pop_task()` (`:224-230` - `drop_task_inline` + `pop_dealloc` + `Cell::set`), and `try_get_result::<R>` (`:158-174`) performs a non-atomic `Option::take`. `StackAllocator::pop_dealloc` (`src/allocator.rs:118`) is a bump-allocator pop with no synchronisation.

**Variant A (concurrent poll of one `Stack`).** `let mut stack = Stack::new(); let mut runner = stack.enter(closure); let f1 = runner.step_async(); let f2 = runner.step_async();` then move `f1`/`f2` to two threads and `block_on` them. Both threads call `drive_top_task(&self)` on the same `Stack`; when the stored task becomes ready both can run `pop_task()`, dropping the same `TaskBox` twice (double free for a heap-owning future) and popping the bump allocator twice; `finish_async` duplicates can additionally take the same `Option<R>` twice. This is plain safe code - `unsafe` is not involved anywhere in the caller.

**Variant B (`!Send` capture crossing threads).** `TreeStack::enter` accepts `Fut: Future + 'a` with no `Send` bound, and `unsafe impl Send for TreeStack`/`Runner` allows the driver (with the future already inside the type-erased store) to be moved to another thread while the creating thread still holds an alias of the captured `!Send` state. A `Rc<RefCell<_>>` is the minimal example; in `surrealdb-core`'s case the payload is a future capturing `&mut tree::Stk`, which is itself `!Send` by construction.

**Why `surrealdb-core` is affected (and why the impl is load-bearing).** `dbs::Executor` stores `stack: TreeStack` (`surrealdb/core/src/dbs/executor.rs:51-63`, constructed at `:141`) and every statement is driven with `self.stack.enter(|stk| s.compute(stk, &self.ctx, &self.opt, None)).finish().await` (`:852,904,912,924`). The closure returns `s.compute(stk, …)`, whose `stk: &mut tree::Stk` argument is held across await points, and `tree::Stk` is deliberately `!Send` (`PhantomData<*mut TreeStack>`, `src/tree/stk.rs:19`) - so the type-erased payload inside that `TreeStack` is `!Send`. Because `dbs::Executor` lives across awaits inside the server's tokio/axum request path, `surrealdb-core`'s query futures must remain `Send`, which they only do because of the unconditional `unsafe impl Send for TreeStack`. Other in-tree users of the same runtime include `api/invocation.rs:48`, `syn/mod.rs:31,55-59` (the SurrealQL parser), `gql/mod.rs:37,73-74`, `doc/event.rs:341` and `doc/lives.rs:113`; `surrealdb/core/src` contains approximately 471 `stk.run(...)` call sites, 112 `TreeStack::new()` and 16 non-tree `Stack::new()`.

**Honest scope.** We did not find an existing in-tree call site that duplicates `StepFuture`/`FinishFuture` or moves a `Runner`/`TreeStack` across threads with a `!Send` capture; the reproduction exercises `reblessive`'s public API directly (as a `surrealdb-core` integration test, so it links the exact 0.4.3 that `surrealdb-core` resolves). It is not remote/black-box triggerable. The defect is nevertheless in `surrealdb-core`'s security-relevant dependency surface: the runtime is on the query hot path and the unsound `Send` assertion is what makes SurrealDB's query futures compile.

**Async specificity (for context).** The defect needs the future-as-value model: the runtime stores futures in a type-erased heap stack and exposes shared-reference driver futures (`&Stack`) that its own `Sync` assertion declares safe to send across threads. Synchronous Rust has no equivalent shape - a `&mut self` driver cannot be duplicated, which is exactly why binding `step_async`/`finish_async` to `&mut Runner` would close variant A. The consequence class (concurrent mutation through a shared reference permitted by an unsound `Sync`) is the same class as `RUSTSEC-2025-0023` (tokio `broadcast` parallel clone) and `futures` `FuturesUnordered`'s `Sync` bound.

**Related but distinct.** `surrealdb/surrealdb#7302` (open) reports a production panic, `Stack still has unresolved futures, did a previous runner leak?` (`reblessive-0.4.3/src/stack/mod.rs:101`), triggered by `--transaction-timeout` cancellation: `Runner::finish(self)` calls `std::mem::forget(self)` (`src/tree/runner.rs:75-82`) and `FinishFuture`/`StepFuture` have **no `Drop`**, so a cancelled statement leaves tasks on the same `Executor` stack and the next `enter` hits the assertion. That is a panic/liveness defect, not the UB reported here; we mention it because it is the same state machine and shows the cancellation path has no cleanup. A draft PR (`#7342`, "Fix transaction timeout stack cleanup") targets it.

**Suggested remediation (upstream).** Remove the unconditional `Send`/`Sync`; expose `Send` only when the erased payload is `Send` and drop `Sync` entirely (e.g. require `Fut: Send` for the `Send` impl), and/or bind `step_async`/`finish_async` to `&mut Runner` so driver futures cannot be duplicated or sent. Adding `Drop` to `FinishFuture`/`StepFuture` (or not `mem::forget`-ing the `Runner` in `finish`) would also remove the cancellation-leak panic from #7302. Downstream, `surrealdb-core` should keep each `TreeStack` confined to a single task/thread and should not replicate the type-erased `Send` assertion; because the `Send` requirement is structural, we expect the practical fix to be an upstream release rather than a SurrealDB-local patch.

**Disclosure.** Not disclosed publicly or to any third party. The complete PoC source and an abridged TSan report are in the collapsible blocks below; the single-threaded control output (`1 passed; 0 failed; 3 ignored`), the complete 636 KB TSan log (11 races) and the raw run are available and will be posted in the advisory thread - the report form has no attachment field.

### PoC

Environment: Linux x86_64, `cargo +nightly-2026-04-14` with `-Zbuild-std --target x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu` and ThreadSanitizer (`--target` is required so `RUSTFLAGS` are not applied to build scripts and proc macros; `-Zbuild-std` is required for TSan).

Minimal trigger (variant A - duplicated `StepFuture`s, two threads, safe code only):

````rust
use futures::executor::block_on;
use reblessive::Stack;

let mut stack = Stack::new();
let owned = String::from("owned by the type-erased future");
let mut runner = stack.enter(move |_stk| async move { drop(owned); });

// `step_async(&mut self) -> StepFuture<'a, R>` - the returned lifetime is the stack
// borrow, not `&mut self`, so a second driver future may coexist.
let f1 = runner.step_async();
let f2 = runner.step_async();

// `Stack: Sync` ⇒ `&Stack: Send` ⇒ both futures are `Send`.
std::thread::scope(|s| {
    s.spawn(move || { let _ = block_on(f1); }); // both threads pop/dealloc the
    s.spawn(move || { let _ = block_on(f2); }); // same task → double free / race
});
````

Variant B (`TreeStack` sends a `!Send` capture across threads):

````rust
use std::cell::RefCell;
use std::rc::Rc;
use futures::executor::block_on;
use reblessive::TreeStack;

let shared = Rc::new(RefCell::new(0usize));
let alias = Rc::clone(&shared);              // alias kept on the creating thread
let mut stack = TreeStack::new();
let captured = Rc::clone(&shared);           // moved into the type-erased future
let runner = stack.enter(move |_stk| async move {
    *captured.borrow_mut() += 1;
    drop(captured);
});
std::thread::scope(|s| {
    s.spawn(move || { let _ = block_on(runner.finish()); }); // `Runner: Send` (unsafe impl)
    for _ in 0..1_000_000 { *alias.borrow_mut() += 1; }      // races Rc/RefCell state
});
````

Run (crate-level integration test; no `mod.rs` or `Cargo.toml` edit needed - `reblessive` and `futures` are normal dependencies of `surrealdb-core`):

````bash
RUSTFLAGS="-Zsanitizer=thread" cargo +nightly-2026-04-14 test -Zbuild-std \
  --target x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu -p surrealdb-core \
  --test f2_reblessive_send_sync_unsound -- --ignored --nocapture --test-threads=1
````

Observed: `running 3 tests` → **11 × `WARNING: ThreadSanitizer: data race`** → `test result: FAILED. 2 passed; 1 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 1 filtered out; finished in 0.85s` (exit status 66) and `ThreadSanitizer: reported 11 warnings`. The first race is on `reblessive`'s own stack state: `Cell<usize>::get` inside `Stack::try_get_result` (`stack/mod.rs:159`) versus `Cell::set` inside `Stack::pop_task` (`stack/mod.rs:228`), called from `drive_top_task` - the "Location" line says `Location is stack of thread T1`, i.e. the `Stack` value itself. In the concurrent-result-take test, the corrupted allocator makes `reblessive` panic at `stack/mod.rs:177:54: called Option::unwrap() on a None value` ("a scoped thread panicked"). A single-threaded control with the identical `Rc`/`RefCell` capture passes under `cargo test` (`1 passed; 0 failed; 3 ignored`), which isolates the failure to concurrent polling / cross-thread movement rather than to `Rc`/`RefCell` use as such. Note that TSan reports races without aborting, so two of the three tests still display `passed`; the `WARNING` lines (and `reported 11 warnings`) are the evidence.

