<summary>PoC - complete source (crate-level integration test, 4 tests; safe code, no `unsafe`)</summary>

````rust
//! F2 PoC - `reblessive 0.4.3` unsound `Send`/`Sync` on its recursive-async runtime.
//!
//! Analysis summary: unsound `Send`/`Sync` assertions in `reblessive 0.4.3`; see the report description.
//!
//! `reblessive` is a **normal, non-optional** dependency of `surrealdb-core`
//! (`surrealdb/core/Cargo.toml:95`, `features = ["tree"]`), pinned by `Cargo.lock`
//! to `0.4.3` (checksum `bbc4a4ea…e73c8c`, verified against the crates.io tarball).
//! SurrealDB drives every SurrealQL statement through it
//! (`dbs/executor.rs:852,904,912,924`) and `dbs::Executor` stores a `TreeStack`
//! field (`dbs/executor.rs:51-63`), so the affected runtime sits on the query hot
//! path; `tree::Stk` is deliberately `!Send`, yet the futures stored inside the
//! stack capture `&mut Stk`, and SurrealDB's query futures stay `Send` only
//! because of the unconditional `unsafe impl Send for TreeStack`.
//!
//! What this file demonstrates - entirely through the **public** `reblessive` API,
//! with **no `unsafe` anywhere**:
//!
//! 1. `unsafe impl Sync for Stack` (`stack/mod.rs:66`) together with
//!    `Runner::step_async(&mut self) -> StepFuture<'a, R>` / `finish_async(&mut self)
//!    -> FinishFuture<'a, R>` (`stack/runner.rs:24,35`; the returned lifetime is the
//!    *stack* borrow, not `&mut self`) lets safe code create multiple driver futures
//!    over one stack, make them `Send` (`&Stack: Send` ⇐ `Stack: Sync`) and poll them
//!    from two threads. Both threads run `drive_top_task(&self)`
//!    (`stack/mod.rs:175-199`), which mutates `Cell`/`UnsafeCell` state and can run
//!    `drop_task_inline` + `pop_dealloc` on the same task twice (`stack/mod.rs:224-230`),
//!    and `try_get_result` (`stack/mod.rs:158-174`) can hand the same `Option<R>` to
//!    both threads - a double drop / double free / torn `Cell` race.
//! 2. `unsafe impl Send for TreeStack` (`tree/mod.rs:34`) and
//!    `unsafe impl<R> Send for Runner<'_, R>` (`tree/runner.rs:64`) let safe code move
//!    a driver whose type-erased future captures `!Send` state (`Rc<RefCell<_>>`) to
//!    another thread while the creating thread keeps an alias of the same `!Send`
//!    state - a non-atomic refcount / borrow-flag data race.
//!
//! # Integration (no `mod.rs` or `Cargo.toml` edit needed)
//!
//! Copy this file to `surrealdb/core/tests/f2_reblessive_send_sync_unsound.rs`.
//! Cargo auto-discovers `tests/*.rs`, and both `reblessive` and `futures` are normal
//! dependencies of `surrealdb-core`, so they are in scope for the test crate.
//!
//! # Running
//!
//! The three UB tests are `#[ignore]`d; `treestack_single_thread_control` runs by
//! default and must pass.
//!
//! ```text
//! # TSan - the right oracle for the data races (Linux, nightly + rust-src)
//! RUSTFLAGS="-Zsanitizer=thread" cargo +nightly-2026-04-14 test -Zbuild-std \
//!   --target x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu -p surrealdb-core \
//!   --test f2_reblessive_send_sync_unsound -- --ignored --nocapture --test-threads=1
//!
//! # ASan - may catch variant A's double free (not the right oracle for races)
//! RUSTFLAGS="-Zsanitizer=address" ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0 cargo +nightly-2026-04-14 test \
//!   -Zbuild-std --target x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu -p surrealdb-core \
//!   --test f2_reblessive_send_sync_unsound -- --ignored --nocapture --test-threads=1
//! ```
//!
//! Miri is not practical for this target: `cargo miri test -p surrealdb-core` would
//! have to interpret SurrealDB's entire dependency tree. TSan is the intended oracle
//! for the data races; ASan may additionally catch variant A's double free.
//!
//! A plain `cargo test` may pass or panic - that is **not** a disproof: the defect
//! is a race, not a deterministic crash.

#![allow(dead_code)]

use std::cell::RefCell;
use std::rc::Rc;

use futures::executor::block_on;
use reblessive::{Stack, TreeStack};

// ===========================================================================
// Variant A: non-tree `Stack` - duplicated driver futures, concurrent poll
// ===========================================================================

/// `unsafe impl Sync for Stack` + `step_async`'s `'a`-lifetime return value let two
/// `StepFuture`s coexist over one stack; `Stack: Sync` makes them `Send`.
///
/// Two threads concurrently `drive_top_task(&self)`: the same `UnsafeCell<StackAllocator>`
/// and `Cell<len>` are read/written unsynchronized, and on `Ready` both threads can run
/// `pop_task()` (drop the same task twice, `pop_dealloc` twice). The stored future owns a
/// heap allocation, so the double drop is a double free.
#[test]
#[ignore = "deliberately triggers UB (data race / double free); run explicitly under TSan or ASan"]
fn stack_sync_concurrent_poll_double_free() {
	let mut stack = Stack::new();

	// A heap-owning future: double drop == double free.
	let owned = String::from("owned by the type-erased future");
	let mut runner = stack.enter(move |_stk| async move {
		std::hint::black_box(&owned);
		drop(owned);
	});

	// `step_async(&mut self) -> StepFuture<'a, R>`: `'a` is the *stack* borrow, so the
	// first call's mutable borrow does not live in the returned value and a second
	// call is legal.
	let f1 = runner.step_async();
	let f2 = runner.step_async();

	// `Stack: Sync` ⇒ `&Stack: Send` ⇒ `StepFuture<'a, ()>: Send`.
	std::thread::scope(|scope| {
		let h1 = scope.spawn(move || {
			let _ = block_on(f1);
		});
		let h2 = scope.spawn(move || {
			let _ = block_on(f2);
		});
		let _ = (h1.join(), h2.join());
	});
}

/// `FinishFuture` is duplicated the same way. `try_get_result::<R>` takes from the same
/// `Option<R>` non-atomically, so both threads can end up owning the same heap value,
/// and both pop the allocator's top.
#[test]
#[ignore = "deliberately triggers UB (data race / double free); run explicitly under TSan or ASan"]
fn stack_sync_concurrent_result_take_double_free() {
	let mut stack = Stack::new();
	let mut runner = stack.enter(|_stk| async move { String::from("result") });

	let f1 = runner.finish_async();
	let f2 = runner.finish_async();

	std::thread::scope(|scope| {
		scope.spawn(move || {
			let _ = block_on(f1);
		});
		scope.spawn(move || {
			let _ = block_on(f2);
		});
	});
}

// ===========================================================================
// Variant B: tree `TreeStack` - `Send` assertion + `!Send` capture
// ===========================================================================

/// `unsafe impl Send for TreeStack` / `unsafe impl<R> Send for Runner<'_, R>`, plus an
/// `enter` bound with no `Send` on `Fut`, let safe code move a `Runner` holding an
/// `Rc<RefCell<_>>` capture to another thread while the creating thread still uses an
/// alias of that `Rc`.
///
/// `Rc`'s refcount and `RefCell`'s borrow flag are non-atomic, so the concurrent access
/// is a data race (an early free of the `Rc` can turn it into a use-after-free).
///
/// This is the shape that matters for SurrealDB: `Executor` moves a `TreeStack` across
/// tokio worker threads and the futures stored inside capture `&mut tree::Stk`, which is
/// explicitly `!Send` (`tree/stk.rs:19`).
#[test]
#[ignore = "deliberately triggers UB (data race); run explicitly under TSan"]
